{:edb/db-before-t 2244 :edb/db-after-t 2245 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535557 50 #inst "2026-10-14T12:09:58.098Z" 13194139535557 17592186045417 true] [17592186052066 1075 #edb/ref 17592186516622 13194139535557 17592186045417 true] [17592186052066 1078 #edb/ref 17592186516622 13194139535557 17592186045417 true] [17592186052067 1075 #edb/ref 17592186516201 13194139535557 17592186045417 true] [17592186052067 1078 #edb/ref 17592186516201 13194139535557 17592186045417 true] [17592186053973 1075 #edb/ref 17592186053975 13194139535557 17592186045417 false] [17592186053973 1075 #edb/ref 17592186053976 13194139535557 17592186045417 false] [17592186053973 1075 #edb/ref 17592186053977 13194139535557 17592186045417 false] [17592186053973 1075 #edb/ref 17592186516265 13194139535557 17592186045417 true] [17592186053973 1075 #edb/ref 17592186516294 13194139535557 17592186045417 true] [17592186053973 1075 #edb/ref 17592186516582 13194139535557 17592186045417 true] [17592186053973 1078 #edb/ref 17592186516294 13194139535557 17592186045417 true] [17592186054520 1071 "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{2}{3} x \\,\\textrm d x$." 13194139535557 17592186045417 false] [17592186054520 1071 "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{2}{3} x \\,\\textrm d x.$" 13194139535557 17592186045417 true] [17592186054520 1074 "We can just take the constant factor out of the integral, and then apply the power rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int\\frac{2}{3}xdx &= \\frac{2}{3}\\intxdx \\\\\n&= \\frac{2}{3}(\\frac{x^{1 + 1}}{1 + 1}) + C \\\\\n&= \\frac{2}{3} \\cdot \\frac{x^{2}}{2} + C \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{3}x^{2} + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535557 17592186045417 false] [17592186054520 1074 "We can just take the constant factor out of the integral, and then apply the power rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2}{3}x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{2}{3}\\int x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}(\\frac{{x}^{1+1}}{1+1})+C \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{2}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535557 17592186045417 true] [17592186054521 1071 "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int 6 \\,\\textrm d x$." 13194139535557 17592186045417 false] [17592186054521 1071 "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int 6 \\,\\textrm d x.$" 13194139535557 17592186045417 true] [17592186054521 1074 "We can just take the constant factor out of the integral, and then apply the power rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int6dx &= 6\\int1dx \\\\\n&= 6\\intx^{0}dx \\\\\n&= 6 \\cdot \\frac{x^{0 + 1}}{0 + 1} + C \\\\\n&= 6 \\cdot x + C \\\\\n&= 6x + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn general, we can see that for any constant $k$, we have\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int k \\,\\textrm d x = kx + C\n$$" 13194139535557 17592186045417 false] [17592186054521 1074 "We can just take the constant factor out of the integral, and then apply the power rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 6\\,\\text{d}x & =6\\int 1\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6\\int {x}^{0}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{0+1}}{0+1}+C \\\\  & =6\\cdot x+C \\\\  & =6x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn general, we can see that for any constant $k,$ we have $\\displaystyle \\int k \\,\\textrm d x = kx + C.$" 13194139535557 17592186045417 true] [17592186054533 1071 "Evaluate $2 \\cdot 3\\,\\textrm{i}$." 13194139535557 17592186045417 false] [17592186054533 1071 "Evaluate $2 \\cdot 3\\,\\textrm{i}.$" 13194139535557 17592186045417 true] [17592186054533 1074 "Multiplying a purely imaginary number by a real number is similar to multiplying two monomials.\n\nFor the given problem, we have\n\n$$\n2\\cdot 3\\,\\textrm{i}\n\t= (2\\cdot 3) \\,\\textrm{i}\n\t= 6\\,\\textrm{i}.\n$$" 13194139535557 17592186045417 true] [17592186054533 1074 "Multiplying a purely imaginary number by a real number is similar to multiplying two monomials.\n\nFor the given problem, we have\n\n$$\n2\\cdot 3\\,\\textrm{i} = (2\\cdot 3) \\,\\textrm{i} = 6\\,\\textrm{i}\n$$" 13194139535557 17592186045417 false] [17592186054534 1071 "Evaluate $2\\,\\textrm{i}\\cdot (-3\\,\\textrm{i})$." 13194139535557 17592186045417 false] [17592186054534 1071 "Evaluate $2\\,\\textrm{i}\\cdot (-3\\,\\textrm{i}).$" 13194139535557 17592186045417 true] [17592186054534 1074 "Multiplying two purely imaginary numbers is similar to multiplying two monomials. We also use the fact that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{i}^2=-1\n$$\n\nFor the given problem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2\\textrm{i} \\cdot (-3\\textrm{i}) &= 2 \\cdot (-3) \\cdot \\textrm{i}^{2} \\\\\n&=-6\\textrm{i}^{2} \\\\\n&=-6 \\cdot (-1) \\\\\n&= 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535557 17592186045417 false] [17592186054534 1074 "Multiplying two purely imaginary numbers is similar to multiplying two monomials. We also use the fact that $\\textrm{i}^2=-1.$\n\nFor the given problem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\,\\text{i}\\cdot (-3\\,\\text{i}) & =2\\cdot (-3)\\cdot {\\text{i}}^{2} \\\\  & =-6\\,{\\text{i}}^{2} \\\\  & =-6\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535557 17592186045417 true] [17592186054535 1071 "Evaluate $6\\,\\textrm{i}\\cdot(4 + 2\\,\\textrm{i})$." 13194139535557 17592186045417 false] [17592186054535 1071 "Evaluate $6\\,\\textrm{i}\\cdot(4 + 2\\,\\textrm{i}).$" 13194139535557 17592186045417 true] [17592186054535 1074 "To multiply a complex number by a purely imaginary number, we use the distributive property and the fact that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{i}^2 = -1\n$$\n\nFor the given problem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n6\\textrm{i} \\cdot (4 + 2\\textrm{i}) &= 6\\textrm{i} \\cdot 4 + 6\\textrm{i} \\cdot 2\\textrm{i} \\\\\n&= 4 \\cdot 6\\textrm{i} + 6 \\cdot 2 \\cdot \\textrm{i}^{2} \\\\\n&= 24\\textrm{i} + 12 \\cdot (-1) \\\\\n&= 24\\textrm{i} - 12 \\\\\n&=-12 + 24\\textrm{i}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535557 17592186045417 false] [17592186054535 1074 "To multiply a complex number by a purely imaginary number, we use the distributive property and the fact that $\\textrm{i}^2 = -1.$\n\nFor the given problem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6\\,\\text{i}\\cdot (4+2\\,\\text{i}) & =6\\,\\text{i}\\cdot 4+6\\,\\text{i}\\cdot 2\\,\\text{i} \\\\  & =4\\cdot 6\\,\\text{i}+6\\cdot 2\\cdot \\,{\\text{i}}^{2} \\\\  & =24\\,\\text{i}+12\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =24\\,\\text{i}-12 \\\\  & =-12+24\\,\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535557 17592186045417 true] [17592186054536 1071 "Evaluate $(1 - 3\\,\\textrm{i})(2 + 4\\, \\textrm{i})$." 13194139535557 17592186045417 false] [17592186054536 1071 "Evaluate $(1 - 3\\,\\textrm{i})(2 + 4\\, \\textrm{i}).$" 13194139535557 17592186045417 true] [17592186054536 1074 "Multiplying two complex numbers is similar to multiplying two binomials. We also use the fact that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{i}^2 = -1\n$$\n\nFor the given problem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(1 - 3\\textrm{i})(2 + 4\\textrm{i}) &= 1 \\cdot 2 + 1 \\cdot 4\\textrm{i} + (-3\\textrm{i}) \\cdot 2 + (-3\\textrm{i}) \\cdot 4\\textrm{i} \\\\\n&= 2 + 4\\textrm{i} - 6\\textrm{i} - 12\\textrm{i}^{2} \\\\\n&= 2 - 2\\textrm{i} - 12 \\cdot (-1) \\\\\n&= 2 - 2\\textrm{i} + 12 \\\\\n&= 14 - 2\\textrm{i}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535557 17592186045417 false] [17592186054536 1074 "Multiplying two complex numbers is similar to multiplying two binomials. We also use the fact that $\\textrm{i}^2 = -1.$\n\nFor the given problem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(1-3\\,\\text{i})(2+4\\,\\text{i}) & =1\\cdot 2+1\\cdot 4\\,\\text{i}+(-3\\,\\text{i})\\cdot 2+(-3\\,\\text{i})\\cdot 4\\,\\text{i} \\\\  & =2+4\\,\\text{i}-6\\,\\text{i}-12\\,{\\text{i}}^{2} \\\\  & =2-2\\,\\text{i}-12\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =2-2\\,\\text{i}+12 \\\\  & =14-2\\,\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535557 17592186045417 true] [17592186054609 1038 #edb/ref 17592186516856 13194139535557 17592186045417 true] [17592186054610 1038 #edb/ref 17592186516872 13194139535557 17592186045417 true] [17592186054611 1038 #edb/ref 17592186516773 13194139535557 17592186045417 true] [17592186054612 1038 #edb/ref 17592186516817 13194139535557 17592186045417 true] [17592186054638 1038 #edb/ref 17592186516764 13194139535557 17592186045417 true] [17592186054730 1038 #edb/ref 17592186516851 13194139535557 17592186045417 true] [17592186054731 1038 #edb/ref 17592186516771 13194139535557 17592186045417 true] [17592186054731 1038 #edb/ref 17592186516839 13194139535557 17592186045417 true] [17592186054732 1038 #edb/ref 17592186516869 13194139535557 17592186045417 true] [17592186054733 1038 #edb/ref 17592186516811 13194139535557 17592186045417 true] [17592186054733 1038 #edb/ref 17592186516865 13194139535557 17592186045417 true] [17592186054895 1050 420.0 13194139535557 17592186045417 true] [17592186054903 1050 420.0 13194139535557 17592186045417 true] [17592186055115 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535557 17592186045417 true] [17592186055115 1071 "$(-4\\,\\text{i})\\cdot (2+3\\,\\text{i})=${{field-1}}$-$\n{{field-2}}$\\text{i}$" 13194139535557 17592186045417 true] [17592186055115 1071 "$(-4\\textrm{i}) \\cdot (2 + 3\\textrm{i}) =$ {{field-1}} $-$\n{{field-2}} $\\textrm{i}$" 13194139535557 17592186045417 false] [17592186055115 1074 "To multiply a complex number by a purely imaginary number, we use the distributive property and the fact that ${i}^{2}=-1.$\n\nFor the given problem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-4\\,\\text{i})\\cdot (2+3\\,\\text{i}) & =(-4\\,\\text{i})\\cdot 2+(-4\\,\\text{i})\\cdot 3\\,\\text{i} \\\\  & =(-4)\\cdot 2\\,\\text{i}+(-4)\\cdot 3\\cdot {\\text{i}}^{2} \\\\  & =-8\\,\\text{i}+(-12)\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =-8\\,\\text{i}+12 \\\\  & =12-8\\,\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535557 17592186045417 true] [17592186055456 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535557 17592186045417 true] [17592186055456 1071 "$6\\,\\text{i}\\cdot (-3)=$" 13194139535557 17592186045417 true] [17592186055456 1071 "What is $6\\textrm{i} \\cdot (-3)$?" 13194139535557 17592186045417 false] [17592186055456 1074 "Multiplying a purely imaginary number by a real number is similar to multiplying two monomials.\n\nFor the given problem, we have\n\n$$\n6\\,\\text{i}\\cdot (-3)=(6\\cdot (-3))\\,\\text{i}=-18\\,\\text{i}.\n$$" 13194139535557 17592186045417 true] [17592186055968 1064 "Multiplying a purely imaginary number by a real number is similar to multiplying two monomials.\n\nFor example, we can multiply the purely imaginary number $3 \\textrm{i}$ by the real number $6$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n6 \\cdot 3\\textrm{i} &= (6 \\cdot 3)\\textrm{i} = 18\\textrm{i}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, we can multiply a complex number by a real number using the distributive property.\n\nFor example, we can multiply the complex number $2-3\\textrm{i}$ by the real number $3$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} 3(2-3\\textrm{i}) &= 3\\cdot2 + 3\\cdot(-3\\textrm{i}) \\\\[3pt] &= 6 + 3\\cdot (-3)\\textrm{i} \\\\[3pt] &= 6 -9\\textrm{i} \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535557 17592186045417 false] [17592186055968 1064 "Multiplying a purely imaginary number by a real number is similar to multiplying two monomials.\n\nFor example, we can multiply the purely imaginary number $3 \\textrm{i}$ by the real number $6$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6\\cdot 3\\text{i} & =(6\\cdot 3)\\text{i}=18\\text{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, we can multiply a complex number by a real number using the distributive property.\n\nFor example, we can multiply the complex number $2-3\\textrm{i}$ by the real number $3$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n3(2-3\\textrm{i}) &= 3\\cdot2 + 3\\cdot(-3\\textrm{i}) \\[3pt]\n\t\t&= 6 + 3\\cdot (-3)\\textrm{i} \\[3pt]\n\t\t&= 6 -9\\textrm{i}\n\t}\n$$" 13194139535557 17592186045417 true] [17592186055972 1064 "Finally, we can multiply two complex numbers just like multiplying two binomials. Again, we use the fact that\n\n$$\n\\mathrm{i}^2=-1\n$$\n\nSo, to multiply $2 + \\textrm{i}$ and $5 + 3\\textrm{i}$, we proceed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} (2+\\textrm{i})(5+3\\textrm{i}) &= 2 (5+3\\textrm{i}) + \\textrm{i} (5+3\\textrm{i}) \\\\ &= 2\\cdot5 + 2\\cdot(3\\textrm{i}) +\\textrm{i}\\cdot 5+ \\textrm{i} \\cdot (3\\textrm{i}) \\\\ &= 10 + 6\\textrm{i} + 5\\textrm{i} +3\\textrm{i}^2 \\\\ &= 10 + 11\\textrm{i} + 3(-1) \\\\ &= 10 + 11\\textrm{i} - 3 \\\\ &= 7 + 11\\textrm{i} \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535557 17592186045417 false] [17592186055972 1064 "Finally, we can multiply two complex numbers just like multiplying two binomials. Again, we use the fact that $\\mathrm{i}^2=-1.$\n\nSo, to multiply $2 + \\textrm{i}$ and $5 + 3\\textrm{i},$ we proceed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t(2+\\textrm{i})(5+3\\textrm{i}) &= 2 (5+3\\textrm{i})  + \\textrm{i} (5+3\\textrm{i}) \\\n\t\t &= 2\\cdot5 + 2\\cdot(3\\textrm{i}) +\\textrm{i}\\cdot 5+ \\textrm{i} \\cdot (3\\textrm{i}) \\\n\t\t&= 10 + 6\\textrm{i} + 5\\textrm{i} +3\\textrm{i}^2 \\\n\t\t&= 10 + 11\\textrm{i} + 3(-1) \\\n\t\t&= 10 + 11\\textrm{i}  - 3 \\\n\t\t&= 7 + 11\\textrm{i}\n\t}\n$$" 13194139535557 17592186045417 true] [17592186055973 1064 "Multiplying two purely imaginary numbers is similar to multiplying two monomials. But we also need to use the fact that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{i}^2=-1\n$$\n\nFor example, to multiply $2\\textrm{i}$ and $3\\textrm{i}$, we proceed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(2\\textrm{i})(3\\textrm{i}) &= 2 \\cdot 3 \\cdot \\textrm{i}^{2} \\\\\n&= 6\\textrm{i}^{2} \\\\\n&= 6(-1) \\\\\n&=-6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nKnowing this, we can also multiply a complex number by a purely imaginary number.\nFor instance, to multiply $2\\textrm{i}$ and $4-3\\textrm{i}$, we use the distributive property and the fact that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{i}^2=-1\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} 2\\textrm{i}(4-3\\textrm{i}) &= 2\\textrm{i}\\cdot 4+2\\textrm{i}\\cdot(-3\\textrm{i}) \\\\[3pt] &=2\\cdot 4\\textrm{i}+2\\cdot(-3)\\textrm{i}^2 \\\\[3pt] &= 8\\textrm{i}+(-6)(-1) \\\\[3pt] &= 8\\textrm{i}+6 \\\\[3pt] &=6+8\\textrm{i} \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535557 17592186045417 false] [17592186055973 1064 "Multiplying two purely imaginary numbers is similar to multiplying two monomials. But we also need to use the fact that $\\textrm{i}^2=-1.$\n\nFor example, to multiply $2\\textrm{i}$ and $3\\textrm{i},$ we proceed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}(2\\text{i})(3\\text{i}) & =2\\cdot 3\\cdot {\\text{i}}^{2} \\\\  & =6{\\text{i}}^{2} \\\\  & =6(-1) \\\\  & =-6\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nKnowing this, we can also multiply a complex number by a purely imaginary number.\nFor instance, to multiply $2\\textrm{i}$ and $4-3\\textrm{i},$ we use the distributive property and the fact that $\\textrm{i}^2=-1\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t2\\textrm{i}(4-3\\textrm{i})\n\t\t\t&= 2\\textrm{i}\\cdot 4+2\\textrm{i}\\cdot(-3\\textrm{i}) \\[3pt]\n&=2\\cdot 4\\textrm{i}+2\\cdot(-3)\\textrm{i}^2\\[3pt]\n&= 8\\textrm{i}+(-6)(-1)\\[3pt]\n&= 8\\textrm{i}+6\\[3pt]\n\n&=6+8\\textrm{i}\n\t}\n$$" 13194139535557 17592186045417 true] [17592186055999 1064 "Suppose we want to find the integral of a power function multiplied by a constant, like $5x^2$. How do we proceed?\n\nAccording to the **constant factor rule**, we can just take the constant factor out of the integral, like this:\n\n$$\n\\int 5 x^2\\, \\textrm{d}x = 5\\cdot \\int x^2 \\,\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nThen, we solve the integral using the power rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n5 \\cdot \\intx^{2}dx &= 5 \\cdot (\\frac{x^{2 + 1}}{2 + 1}) + C \\\\\n&= \\frac{5}{3}x^{3} + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can use this trick in general. Whenever the integrand is a function $f(x)$ multiplied by a constant $k$, we can simplify as\n\n$$\n\\int k f(x)\\, \\textrm{d}x = k \\int f(x) \\,\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\n**Watch out!** We can only take *constant factors* out of integrals. We *cannot* take any variables out of integrals." 13194139535557 17592186045417 false] [17592186055999 1064 "Suppose we want to find the integral of a power function multiplied by a constant, like $5x^2.$ How do we proceed?\n\nAccording to the **constant factor rule**, we can just take the constant factor out of the integral, like this:\n\n$$\n\\int 5 x^2\\, \\textrm{d}x = 5\\cdot \\int x^2 \\,\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nThen, we solve the integral using the power rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5\\cdot \\int {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & =5\\cdot (\\frac{{x}^{2+1}}{2+1})+C \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}{x}^{3}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can use this trick in general. Whenever the integrand is a function $f(x)$ multiplied by a constant $k,$ we can simplify as\n\n$$\n\\int k f(x)\\, \\textrm{d}x = k \\int f(x) \\,\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\n**Watch out!** We can only take *constant factors* out of integrals. We *cannot* take any variables out of integrals." 13194139535557 17592186045417 true] [17592186059824 1038 #edb/ref 17592186516778 13194139535557 17592186045417 true] [17592186059825 1038 #edb/ref 17592186516852 13194139535557 17592186045417 true] [17592186066342 1075 #edb/ref 17592186516512 13194139535557 17592186045417 true] [17592186066342 1078 #edb/ref 17592186516512 13194139535557 17592186045417 true] [17592186068032 1078 #edb/ref 17592186068034 13194139535557 17592186045417 true] [17592186068555 1071 "The equation $\\log_3{9} = 2$ is equivalent to $b^2 = 9$. What is the value of $b$?" 13194139535557 17592186045417 false] [17592186068555 1071 "The equation $\\log_3{9} = 2$ is equivalent to $b^2 = 9.$ What is the value of $b?$" 13194139535557 17592186045417 true] [17592186068555 1074 "The logarithm of $a$ with base $b$ is defined as follows:\n\n$\\log_{b}{a} = x$ is equivalent to $a = b^x$\n\nIf we compare $\\log_3{9} = 2$ with $\\log_{b}{a} = x,$ we get $a = 9,$ $b = 3,$ and $x = 2.$\n\nTherefore, the given equation is equivalent to $3^2 = 9.$ Consequently, $b = 3.$" 13194139535557 17592186045417 true] [17592186068555 1074 "The logarithm of $a$ with base $b$ is defined as follows:\n\n> $\\log_{b}{a} = x$ is equivalent to $a = b^x$\n\nIf we compare\n\n$$\n\\log_3{9} = 2\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\n\\log_{b}{a} = x\n$$\n\nwe get $a = 9$, $b = 3$, and $x = 2$.\n\nTherefore, the given equation is equivalent to $3^2 = 9$. Consequently, $b = 3$." 13194139535557 17592186045417 false] [17592186068556 1071 "The equation $\\log_{6}{1} = 0$ is equivalent to $b^0 = 1$. What is the value of $b$?" 13194139535557 17592186045417 false] [17592186068556 1071 "The equation $\\log_{6}{1} = 0$ is equivalent to $b^0 = 1.$ What is the value of $b?$" 13194139535557 17592186045417 true] [17592186068556 1074 "The logarithm of $a$ with base $b$ is defined as follows:\n\n$\\log_{b}{a} = x$ is equivalent to $a = b^x$\n\nIf we compare $\\log_{6}{1} = 0$ with $\\log_{b}{a} = x,$ we get $a = 1,$ $b = 6,$ and $x = 0.$\n\nTherefore, the given equation is equivalent to $6^0 = 1.$ Consequently, $b = 6.$" 13194139535557 17592186045417 true] [17592186068556 1074 "The logarithm of $a$ with base $b$ is defined as follows:\n\n> $\\log_{b}{a} = x$ is equivalent to $a = b^x$\n\nIf we compare\n\n$$\n\\log_{6}{1} = 0\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\n\\log_{b}{a} = x\n$$\n\nwe get $a = 1$, $b = 6$, and $x = 0$.\n\nTherefore, the given equation is equivalent to $6^0 = 1$. Consequently, $b = 6$." 13194139535557 17592186045417 false] [17592186068557 1071 "The equation $\\log_{16}{4} = \\dfrac{1}{2}$ is equivalent to $\\sqrt{b} = 4$. What is the value of $b$?" 13194139535557 17592186045417 false] [17592186068557 1071 "The equation $\\log_{16}{4} = \\dfrac{1}{2}$ is equivalent to $\\sqrt{b} = 4.$ What is the value of $b?$" 13194139535557 17592186045417 true] [17592186068557 1074 "The logarithm of $a$ with base $b$ is defined as follows:\n\n$\\log_{b}{a} = x$ is equivalent to $a = b^x$\n\nIf we compare $\\log_{16}{4} = \\dfrac{1}{2}$ with $\\log_{b}{a} = x,$ we get $a = 4,$ $b = 16,$ and $x = \\dfrac{1}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the given equation is equivalent to $16^{1/2} = 4,$ which is also equivalent to $\\sqrt{16} = 4.$ Consequently, $b = 16.$" 13194139535557 17592186045417 true] [17592186068557 1074 "The logarithm of $a$ with base $b$ is defined as follows:\n\n> $\\log_{b}{a} = x$ is equivalent to $a = b^x$\n\nIf we compare\n\n$$\n\\log_{16}{4} = \\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\n\\log_{b}{a} = x\n$$\n\nwe get $a = 4$, $b = 16$, and\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n16^{1/2} = 4\n$$\n\nwhich is also equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{16} = 4\n$$\n\nConsequently, $b = 16$." 13194139535557 17592186045417 false] [17592186068558 1071 "The equation $\\log_{2}\\left( \\dfrac{1}{8} \\right) = -3$ is equivalent to $b^{-3} = \\dfrac{1}{8}.$ What is the value of $b?$" 13194139535557 17592186045417 true] [17592186068558 1071 "The equation $\\log_{2}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{8} \\right) = -3$ is equivalent to $b^{-3} = \\dfrac{1}{8}$. What is the value of $b$?" 13194139535557 17592186045417 false] [17592186068558 1074 "The logarithm of $a$ with base $b$ is defined as follows:\n\n$\\log_{b}{a} = x$ is equivalent to $a = b^x$\n\nIf we compare $\\log_{2}\\left( \\dfrac{1}{8} \\right) = -3$ with $\\log_{b}{a} = x,$ we get $a = \\dfrac{1}{8},$ $b = 2,$ and $x = -3.$\n\nTherefore, the given equation is equivalent to $2^{-3} = \\dfrac{1}{8}.$ Consequently, $b = 2.$" 13194139535557 17592186045417 true] [17592186068558 1074 "The logarithm of $a$ with base $b$ is defined as follows:\n\n> $\\log_{b}{a} = x$ is equivalent to $a = b^x$\n\nIf we compare\n\n$$\n\\log_{2}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{8} \\right) = -3\n$$\n\nwith\n\n$$\n\\log_{b}{a} = x\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\na = \\dfrac{1}{8}\n$$\n\n$b = 2$, and $x = -3$.\n\nTherefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n2^{-3} = \\dfrac{1}{8}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $b = 2$." 13194139535557 17592186045417 false] [17592186068869 1038 #edb/ref 17592186516810 13194139535557 17592186045417 true] [17592186068869 1038 #edb/ref 17592186516834 13194139535557 17592186045417 true] [17592186068870 1038 #edb/ref 17592186516788 13194139535557 17592186045417 true] [17592186068871 1038 #edb/ref 17592186516774 13194139535557 17592186045417 true] [17592186068872 1038 #edb/ref 17592186516793 13194139535557 17592186045417 true] [17592186068872 1038 #edb/ref 17592186516849 13194139535557 17592186045417 true] [17592186068984 1050 420.0 13194139535557 17592186045417 true] [17592186069191 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535557 17592186045417 true] [17592186069191 1071 "If the equation $\\log_{25} 5 = \\frac{1}{2}$ is equivalent to $\\sqrt{b} = 5$, then $b =$ {{field-1}}." 13194139535557 17592186045417 false] [17592186069191 1071 "If the equation ${log}_{25}⁡5=\\frac{1}{2}$ is equivalent to $\\sqrt{b}=5,$  then $b=${{field-1}}." 13194139535557 17592186045417 true] [17592186069191 1074 "The logarithm of $a$ with base $b$ is defined as follows:\n\n${log}_{b}⁡a=x$ is equivalent to $a={b}^{x}$\n\nIf we compare ${log}_{25}⁡5=\\frac{1}{2}$ with ${log}_{b}⁡a=x,$ we get $a=5,$ $b=25,$ and $x=\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the given equation is equivalent to ${25}^{1/2}=5,$ which is also equivalent to $\\sqrt{25}=5.$ Consequently, $b=25.$" 13194139535557 17592186045417 true] [17592186069477 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535557 17592186045417 true] [17592186069477 1071 "The equation $\\log_{5} 5 = 1$ is equivalent to $b^{1} = 5$. What is the value of $b$?" 13194139535557 17592186045417 false] [17592186069477 1071 "The equation ${log}_{5}⁡5=1$ is equivalent to ${b}^{1}=5.$ What is the value of $b?$" 13194139535557 17592186045417 true] [17592186069477 1074 "The logarithm of $a$ with base $b$ is defined as follows:\n\n${log}_{b}⁡a=x$ is equivalent to $a={b}^{x}$\n\nIf we compare ${log}_{5}⁡5=1$ with ${log}_{b}⁡a=x,$ we get $a=5,$ $b=5,$ and $x=1.$\n\nTherefore, the given equation is equivalent to ${5}^{1}=5.$ Consequently, $b=5.$" 13194139535557 17592186045417 true] [17592186069965 1064 "So far, we have expressed an exponential equation in logarithm form. We can also go the other way, and express a logarithmic equation in exponential form.\n\nRemember, the base of the logarithm is always the base of the exponential expression.\nFor instance, if we know that\n\n$$\n\\log_{\\color{blue}{2}}(8) = {\\color{red}{3}}\n$$\n\nwe could write this using an exponent as\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{2}}^{\\color{red}{3}} = 8\n$$\n\nIn general, the logarithm of $a$ with base $b$ is defined as follows:\n\n> $\\log_{\\color{blue}b}{a} = {\\color{red}x}$ is equivalent to $a = {\\color{blue}b}^{\\color{red}x}$." 13194139535557 17592186045417 false] [17592186069965 1064 "So far, we have expressed an exponential equation in logarithm form. We can also go the other way, and express a logarithmic equation in exponential form.\n\nRemember, the base of the logarithm is always the base of the exponential expression.\nFor instance, if we know that $\\log_{\\color{blue}{2}}(8) = {\\color{red}{3}},$ we could write this using an exponent as\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{2}}^{\\color{red}{3}} = 8.\n$$\n\nIn general, the logarithm of $a$ with base $b$ is defined as follows:\n\n$\\log_{\\color{blue}b}{a} = {\\color{red}x}$ is equivalent to $a = {\\color{blue}b}^{\\color{red}x}.$" 13194139535557 17592186045417 true] [17592186070354 1078 #edb/ref 17592186070359 13194139535557 17592186045417 true] [17592186073598 1074 "Given two similar polygons, the similarity ratio is the ratio of any two corresponding sides.\n\nNotice that, for example, $\\overline{LM}$ is the longest side of $NLMP$. Hence, it corresponds to the longest side of $ABCD$, which is $\\overline{BC}$.\n\nTherefore, the similarity ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nk &= \\frac{BC}{LM} \\\\\n&= \\frac{33}{22} \\\\\n&= \\frac{3}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** The ratio of the smaller polygon to the larger polygon would be\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{\\left(\\dfrac{3}{2}\\right)}=\\dfrac{2}{3}\n$$" 13194139535557 17592186045417 false] [17592186073598 1074 "Given two similar polygons, the similarity ratio is the ratio of any two corresponding sides. \n\nNotice that, for example, $\\overline{LM}$ is the longest side of $NLMP.$ Hence, it corresponds to the longest side of $ABCD,$ which is  $\\overline{BC}.$ \n\nTherefore, the similarity ratio is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k & =\\frac{BC}{LM} \\\\  & =\\frac{33}{22} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** The ratio of the smaller polygon to the larger polygon would be $\\dfrac{1}{\\left(\\dfrac{3}{2}\\right)}=\\dfrac{2}{3}.$" 13194139535557 17592186045417 true] [17592186073599 1071 "Which of the following quadrilaterals are similar to $\\mathcal{R}$ (other than $\\mathcal R$ itself)?\n\n![](images/5a/5a7c9208a16a639ef6b08855a6caaacb39c121dd4ccf45271d98fcc70f1a8fed.png)" 13194139535557 17592186045417 false] [17592186073599 1071 "Which of the following quadrilaterals are similar to $\\mathcal{R}$ (other than $\\mathcal R$ itself)? \n\n![](images/5a/5a7c9208a16a639ef6b08855a6caaacb39c121dd4ccf45271d98fcc70f1a8fed.png)" 13194139535557 17592186045417 true] [17592186073599 1074 "First, notice that $\\mathcal{R}$ is a rectangle, meaning all the angles measure $90^\\circ$, and it has $2$ pairs of parallel congruent sides.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given polygons.\n\n- $\\mathcal{Q}$ is *not* similar to $\\mathcal{R}$. The polygon $\\mathcal{Q}$ is a rectangle, and the corresponding angles are congruent, but the ratios of the lengths of the corresponding sides are not the same:\n$\\dfrac{8}{6}\\neq\\dfrac{3}{3}$\n- $\\mathcal{P}$ is similar to $\\mathcal{R}$. Indeed, $\\mathcal{P}$ is a rectangle. Furthermore, the corresponding angles are congruent, and the ratios of the lengths of the corresponding sides are the same:\n$\\dfrac{4}{6}=\\dfrac{2}{3}$\n- $\\mathcal{S}$ is *not* similar to $\\mathcal{R}$ because $\\mathcal{S}$ is not a rectangle (it's a parallelogram).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$\\mathcal{P}$ only.\"" 13194139535557 17592186045417 false] [17592186073599 1074 "First, notice that $\\mathcal{R}$ is a rectangle, meaning all the angles measure $90^\\circ,$ and it has $2$ pairs of parallel congruent sides.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given polygons.\n\n- $\\mathcal{Q}$ is *not* similar to $\\mathcal{R}.$ The polygon $\\mathcal{Q}$ is a rectangle, and the corresponding angles are congruent, but the ratios of the lengths of the corresponding sides are not the same: \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{8}{6}\\neq\\dfrac{3}{3}\n$$\n\n- $\\mathcal{P}$ is similar to $\\mathcal{R}.$ Indeed, $\\mathcal{P}$ is a rectangle. Furthermore, the corresponding angles are congruent, and the ratios of the lengths of the corresponding sides are the same:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4}{6}=\\dfrac{2}{3}\n$$\n\n- $\\mathcal{S}$ is *not* similar to $\\mathcal{R}$ because $\\mathcal{S}$ is not a rectangle (it's a parallelogram).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$\\mathcal{P}$ only.\"" 13194139535557 17592186045417 true] [17592186073600 1074 "First, let's find the measures of unknown angles. Since the sum of the measures of internal angles of a quadrilateral is $360^\\circ$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠A + m∠B + m∠C + m∠D &= 360^{∘} \\\\\n55^{∘} + m∠B + 90^{∘} + 125^{∘} &= 360^{∘} \\\\\nm∠B + 270^{∘} &= 360^{∘} \\\\\nm∠B &= 90^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠K + m∠L + m∠M + m∠N &= 360^{∘} \\\\\n55^{∘} + 90^{∘} + m∠M + 125^{∘} &= 360^{∘} \\\\\nm∠M + 270^{∘} &= 360^{∘} \\\\\nm∠M &= 90^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the quadrilaterals are similar, and\n\n$$\n∠A≅∠K, ∠D≅∠N, ∠C≅∠L≅∠B≅∠M\n$$\n\nSo far, we have that\n\n- $A$ corresponds to $K$, and\n- $D$ corresponds to $N$.\n\nFrom the angle measurements, it's unclear whether $C$ corresponds to $L$ or $M$ (these are all right angles). However, $C$ sits between a $125^\\circ$ angle and a $90^\\circ$ angle, and so does $M$ (whereas $L$ does not). So, we also have that\n\n- $C$ corresponds to $M$, and\n- $B$ corresponds to $L$.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that a correct similarity statement is\n$ABCD \\sim KLMN$." 13194139535557 17592186045417 false] [17592186073600 1074 "First, let's find the measures of unknown angles. Since the sum of the measures of internal angles of a quadrilateral is $360^\\circ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠A+m∠B+m∠C+m∠D & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ {55}^{∘}+m∠B+{90}^{∘}+{125}^{∘} & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ m∠B+{270}^{∘} & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ m∠B & ={90}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠K+m∠L+m∠M+m∠N & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ {55}^{∘}+{90}^{∘}+m∠M+{125}^{∘} & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ m∠M+{270}^{∘} & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ m∠M & ={90}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the quadrilaterals are similar, and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∠A≅∠K,\\,∠D≅∠N,\\,∠C≅∠L≅∠B≅∠M.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo far, we have that \n\n- $A$ corresponds to $K,$ and\n\n- $D$ corresponds to $N.$\n\nFrom the angle measurements, it's unclear whether $C$ corresponds to $L$ or $M$ (these are all right angles). However, $C$ sits between a $125^\\circ$ angle and a $90^\\circ$ angle, and so does $M$ (whereas $L$ does not). So, we also have that \n\n- $C$ corresponds to $M,$ and\n\n- $B$ corresponds to $L.$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that a correct similarity statement is \n\n$$\nABCD \\sim  KLMN.\n$$" 13194139535557 17592186045417 true] [17592186073601 1071 "Given that $ABCD \\sim A'B'C'D'$, $AB = 3\\,\\mathrm{ft}$, and $BC = 4\\,\\mathrm{ft}$,\nwhich of the following could be the values of $A'B'$ and $B'C'$?\n\n1. ${A'B'} = 15\\, \\mathrm{ft}, \\, {B'C'} = 20\\, \\mathrm{ft}$\n2. ${A'B'} = 30\\, \\mathrm{ft}, \\, {B'C'} = 44\\, \\mathrm{ft}$\n3. ${A'B'} = 18\\, \\mathrm{ft}, \\, {B'C'} = 24\\, \\mathrm{ft}$" 13194139535557 17592186045417 false] [17592186073601 1071 "Given that $ABCD \\sim A'B'C'D',$ $AB = 3\\,\\mathrm{ft},$ and $BC = 4\\,\\mathrm{ft},$\nwhich of the following could be the values of $A'B'$ and $B'C'?$\n\nI. ${A'B'} = 15\\, \\mathrm{ft}, \\, {B'C'} = 20\\, \\mathrm{ft}$\n\nII. ${A'B'} = 30\\, \\mathrm{ft}, \\, {B'C'} = 44\\, \\mathrm{ft}$\n\nIII. ${A'B'} = 18\\, \\mathrm{ft}, \\, {B'C'} = 24\\, \\mathrm{ft}$" 13194139535557 17592186045417 true] [17592186073601 1074 "For similar polygons, the ratios of lengths of the corresponding sides should be the same.\n\n- For option I, the ratios are the same:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{AB}{{A}^{′}{B}^{′}} & =\\frac{3}{15}=\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ \\frac{BC}{{B}^{′}{C}^{′}} & =\\frac{4}{20}=\\frac{1}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, option I indeed gives possible 