 when the graph $y=g'(x)$ changes its sign. Checking the first derivative at the points $A$ and $B$, we see that\n\n- the point $A$, at $x=1.5$, is a relative maximum of $g(x)$ since the derivative $g'(x)$ switches from positive to negative, and\n- the point $B$, at $x=5$, is a relative minimum of $g(x)$ since the derivative $g'(x)$ switches from negative to positive.\n\nTherefore, statements I and II are true.\n\nNow, an inflection point may occur where the second derivative $y=g''(x)$ is zero (the tangent line to $y=g'(x)$ is horizontal) or $y=g''(x)$ does not exist (a rapid change in the slope). Indeed, at the point $x=3.5$, which we label as $C$, we see that the slope of $g'(x)$ is zero, meaning that $g''(x)=0$.\n\n![](images/e4/e4e945f8e45cd67982246dbd1798f3958eff114f7dc04ff98c3a30f5f570eff9.png)\n\nWe obtain an inflection point if the slope of the tangent line to $y=g'(x)$ changes its sign. Thus, we have an inflection point at $C$, where the slope goes from negative to positive. Therefore, statement III is true.\n\nIn conclusion, all three statements are true." 13194139535557 17592186045417 false] [17592186175765 1074 "First, notice that $y=g'(x)$ intersects the $x$-axis at the points $A$ and $B,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/fb/fbfcc5d729dea273d9b60c2ef80e10696aa0631698a7cb4ff009f54fb59b1ea3.png)\n\nAn extremum of $y=g(x)$ occurs when the graph $y=g'(x)$ changes its sign. Checking the first derivative at the points $A$ and $B,$ we see that\n\n- the point $A,$ at $x=1.5,$ is a relative maximum of $g(x)$ since the derivative $g'(x)$ switches from positive to negative, and\n\n- the point $B,$ at $x=5,$ is a relative minimum of $g(x)$ since the derivative $g'(x)$ switches from negative to positive.\n\nTherefore, statements I and II are true.\n\nNow, an inflection point may occur where the second derivative $y=g''(x)$ is zero (the tangent line to $y=g'(x)$ is horizontal) or $y=g''(x)$ does not exist (a rapid change in the slope). Indeed, at the point $x=3.5,$ which we label as $C,$ we see that the slope of $g'(x)$ is zero, meaning that $g''(x)=0.$\n\n![](images/e4/e4e945f8e45cd67982246dbd1798f3958eff114f7dc04ff98c3a30f5f570eff9.png)\n\nWe obtain an inflection point if the slope of the tangent line to $y=g'(x)$ changes its sign. Thus, we have an inflection point at $C$, where the slope goes from negative to positive. Therefore, statement III is true.\n\nIn conclusion, all three statements are true." 13194139535557 17592186045417 true] [17592186175796 1071 "The graph of $y=f'(x)$, the derivative of $y=f(x)$, is defined on the interval $[-2,3]$ as shown below. Find all of the inflection points of $y=f(x)$ for $x\\in(-2,3)$.\n\n![](images/1d/1da07563588809f268054c88f684f47c701f4daeeb2bfdcb5c01d3f4d9aaa397.png)" 13194139535557 17592186045417 false] [17592186175796 1071 "The graph of $y=f'(x)$, the derivative of $y=f(x)$, is defined on the interval $[-2,3]$ as shown below. Find all of the inflection points of $y=f(x)$ for $x\\in(-2,3).$ \n\n![](images/1d/1da07563588809f268054c88f684f47c701f4daeeb2bfdcb5c01d3f4d9aaa397.png)" 13194139535557 17592186045417 true] [17592186175796 1074 "First, note that $f'(x)$ exists at every point on the interval $x\\in [-2,3]$.\n\nWe need to investigate the behavior of $f''(x)$. We recall the following:\n\n- When $f'(x)$ is increasing, we have $f''(x)>0$.\n- When $f'(x)$ is decreasing, we have $f''(x)< 0$.\n\nThe function $f(x)$ has an inflection point at $x=a$, if the following two conditions are satisfied:\n\n- $f''(x)=0$ or $f''(x)$ is undefined, and\n- $f''(x)$ changes its sign (from negative to positive or vice versa) at that point.\n\nWe summarize the information from the given graph in the table below:\n\n| $x$ | $(-2,-1)$ | $-1$ | $(-1,1)$ | $1$ | $(1,3)$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $f'$ | $\\searrow$ |  | $\\searrow$ |  | $\\nearrow$ |\n| $f''$ | $-$ | $\\text{not def.}$ | $-$ | $\\text{not def.}$ | $+$ |\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f''(x)$ changes its sign at the point $x=1$. Therefore, $x=1$ is a point of inflection of $y=f(x)$.\n\nNote that the sign of $f''(x)$ does not change at $x=-1$, and so this is *not* a point of inflection." 13194139535557 17592186045417 false] [17592186175796 1074 "First, note that $f'(x)$ exists at every point on the interval $x\\in [-2,3].$\n\nWe need to investigate the behavior of $f''(x).$ We recall the following:\n\n- When $f'(x)$ is increasing, we have $f''(x)>0.$\n\n- When $f'(x)$ is decreasing, we have $f''(x)<       0.$\n\nThe function $f(x)$ has an inflection point at $x=a,$ if the following two conditions are satisfied:\n\n- $f''(x)=0$ or $f''(x)$ is undefined, and\n\n- $f''(x)$ changes its sign (from negative to positive or vice versa) at that point.\n\nWe summarize the information from the given graph in the table below:\n\n| $x$ | $(-2,-1)$ | $-1$ | $(-1,1)$ | $1$ | $(1,3)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f'$ | $\\searrow$ |  | $\\searrow$ |  | $\\nearrow$ |\n| $f''$ | $-$ | $\\text{not def.}$ | $-$ | $\\text{not def.}$ | $+$ |\n\nFrom the table, we see that $f''(x)$ changes its sign at the point $x=1.$ Therefore, $x=1$ is a point of inflection of $y=f(x).$\n\nNote that the sign of $f''(x)$ does not change at $x=-1,$ and so this is *not* a point of inflection." 13194139535557 17592186045417 true] [17592186175900 1038 #edb/ref 17592186516781 13194139535557 17592186045417 true] [17592186175900 1038 #edb/ref 17592186516807 13194139535557 17592186045417 true] [17592186175900 1038 #edb/ref 17592186516808 13194139535557 17592186045417 true] [17592186175900 1038 #edb/ref 17592186516826 13194139535557 17592186045417 true] [17592186175900 1038 #edb/ref 17592186516841 13194139535557 17592186045417 true] [17592186175947 1038 #edb/ref 17592186516776 13194139535557 17592186045417 true] [17592186175947 1038 #edb/ref 17592186516833 13194139535557 17592186045417 true] [17592186175947 1038 #edb/ref 17592186516837 13194139535557 17592186045417 true] [17592186175947 1038 #edb/ref 17592186516857 13194139535557 17592186045417 true] [17592186175957 1038 #edb/ref 17592186516840 13194139535557 17592186045417 true] [17592186175957 1038 #edb/ref 17592186516842 13194139535557 17592186045417 true] [17592186176020 1038 #edb/ref 17592186516827 13194139535557 17592186045417 true] [17592186176020 1038 #edb/ref 17592186516864 13194139535557 17592186045417 true] [17592186176100 1050 480.0 13194139535557 17592186045417 true] [17592186176305 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535557 17592186045417 true] [17592186176305 1071 "Consider the following differential equation for the function $y = y(t)$:\n\n$y^{′} = 1000y - 10y^{2}, y(0) = 40$\n\nWhat is $lim_(t → ∞)y(t)$?\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139535557 17592186045417 false] [17592186176305 1071 "Consider the following differential equation for the function $y=y(t):$\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=1\\,000y-10{y}^{2},\\,y(0)=40\n$$\n\nWhat is ${lim}_{t\\to \\infty }y(t)?$" 13194139535557 17592186045417 true] [17592186176305 1074 "A logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=ky(a-y),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>y$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nA diagram showing a typical logistic growth curve is shown below.\n\n![](images/57/57f4b604c546daed4f1cb3895191fae0ccac33b5c17aac705f203f0a837c7e69.png)\n\nFirst, let's factor the right-hand side of the given equation. This gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=10y(100-y),\\,y(0)=40.\n$$\n\nFrom here, we see that $a=100.$ Hence, the carrying capacity is $100.$\n\nFor a given logistic growth model, $y\\to a$ as $t\\to \\infty .$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{t\\to \\infty }y(t)=100.\n$$" 13194139535557 17592186045417 true] [17592186176574 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535557 17592186045417 true] [17592186176574 1071 "![](images/19/199a0809c86f57743fe954d1221656198f92339f146722928b46e598680d299f.png)\n\nThe graph of $y = f^{′}(x)$, the derivative of $y = f(x)$, is defined on the interval $[- 3, 3]$ as shown above. Find all of the inflection points of $y = f(x)$ for $x ∈ (-3, 3)$." 13194139535557 17592186045417 false] [17592186176574 1071 "![](images/19/199a0809c86f57743fe954d1221656198f92339f146722928b46e598680d299f.png)\n\nThe graph of $y={f}^{′}(x)$, the derivative of $y=f(x)$, is defined on the interval $[-3,3]$ as shown above. Find all of the inflection points of $y=f(x)$ for $x\\in (-3,3).$" 13194139535557 17592186045417 true] [17592186176574 1074 "First, note that ${f}^{′}(x)$ exists at every point on the interval $x\\in [-3,3].$\n\nWe need to investigate the behavior of ${f}^{″}(x).$ We recall the following:\n\n- When ${f}^{′}(x)$ is increasing, we have ${f}^{″}(x)>0.$\n\n- When ${f}^{′}(x)$ is decreasing, we have ${f}^{″}(x)<0.$\n\nThe function $f(x)$ has an inflection point at $x=a,$ if the following two conditions are satisfied:\n\n- ${f}^{″}(x)=0$ or ${f}^{″}(x)$ is undefined, and\n\n- ${f}^{″}(x)$ changes its sign (from negative to positive or vice versa) at that point.\n\nWe summarize the information from the given graph in the table below:\n\n| $x$ | $(-3,-1)$ | $-1$ | $(-1,1)$ | $1$ | $(1,3)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $↗$ |  | $↘$ |  | $↗$ |\n| ${f}^{″}$ | $+$ | $\\text{not def.}$ | $-$ | $\\text{not def.}$ | $+$ |\n\nFrom the table, we conclude that ${f}^{″}(x)$ changes its sign at the points $x=-1$ and $x=1.$ Therefore, $x=-1$ and $x=1$ are the points of inflection of $y=f(x).$" 13194139535557 17592186045417 true] [17592186177063 1064 "Recall that we can use the second derivative to determine the concavity of a differentiable function $f(x)$:\n\n- $f(x)$ is concave up on an interval $(a,b)$ if $f''(x) > 0$ on $(a,b)$, while\n- $f(x)$ is concave down on an interval $(a,b)$ if $f''(x) < 0$ on $(a,b)$.\n\nSo, to determine intervals of concavity of $f(x)$ by looking at the graph of $f'(x)$, we need to pay attention to where $f'(x)$ is increasing or decreasing.\n\n- If $f'(x)$ is increasing, then $f''(x)>0$, which means $f(x)$ is concave up.![](images/8a/8ac780a2dfa4d6c32449b750ff236568764de920d38e363d1badf4c7324c9c94.png)\n\n- If $f'(x)$ is decreasing, then $f''(x)<0$, which means $f(x)$ is concave down.\n\n![](images/2b/2bcc5e92916f582dc75450241c0223b55dab287d24e06b55e4d8fde56c7f927c.png)\n\nAlso, recall that $f(x)$ has an inflection point at $x=a$ if $f''(x)$ changes its sign around $x=a$. So, to determine the inflection points of $f(x)$ by looking at a graph of $f'(x)$, we need to pay attention to where $f'(x)$ switches from increasing to decreasing (or vice versa).\n\nIn other words, we need to pay attention to relative extrema on the graph of $f'(x)$.\n\n![](images/68/68a0a5523690eceb319bff14916528b6c1727d68e393373553bc42fbf6be6f16.png)" 13194139535557 17592186045417 false] [17592186177063 1064 "Recall that we can use the second derivative to determine the concavity of a differentiable function $f(x)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n- $f(x)$ is concave up on an interval $(a,b)$ if $f''(x) > 0$ on $(a,b),$ while\n\n- $f(x)$ is concave down on an interval $(a,b)$ if $f''(x) < 0$ on $(a,b).$\n\nSo, to determine intervals of concavity of $f(x)$ by looking at the graph of $f'(x),$ we need to pay attention to where $f'(x)$ is increasing or decreasing.\n\n- If $f'(x)$ is increasing, then $f''(x)>0,$ which means $f(x)$ is concave up.\n\n![](images/8a/8ac780a2dfa4d6c32449b750ff236568764de920d38e363d1badf4c7324c9c94.png)\n\n- If $f'(x)$ is decreasing, then $f''(x)<0,$ which means $f(x)$ is concave down.\n\n![](images/2b/2bcc5e92916f582dc75450241c0223b55dab287d24e06b55e4d8fde56c7f927c.png)\n\nAlso, recall that $f(x)$ has an inflection point at $x=a$ if $f''(x)$ changes its sign around $x=a.$ So, to determine the inflection points of $f(x)$ by looking at a graph of $f'(x),$ we need to pay attention to where $f'(x)$ switches from increasing to decreasing (or vice versa). \n\nIn other words, we need to pay attention to relative extrema on the graph of $f'(x).$\n\n![](images/68/68a0a5523690eceb319bff14916528b6c1727d68e393373553bc42fbf6be6f16.png)" 13194139535557 17592186045417 true] [17592186179649 1038 #edb/ref 17592186516785 13194139535557 17592186045417 true] [17592186179817 1038 #edb/ref 17592186516820 13194139535557 17592186045417 true] [17592186179818 1038 #edb/ref 17592186516845 13194139535557 17592186045417 true] [17592186184192 1038 #edb/ref 17592186516838 13194139535557 17592186045417 true] [17592186186378 1075 #edb/ref 17592186186379 13194139535557 17592186045417 false] [17592186186378 1075 #edb/ref 17592186186380 13194139535557 17592186045417 false] [17592186186378 1075 #edb/ref 17592186186381 13194139535557 17592186045417 false] [17592186186378 1075 #edb/ref 17592186186382 13194139535557 17592186045417 false] [17592186186378 1075 #edb/ref 17592186186383 13194139535557 17592186045417 false] [17592186186378 1075 #edb/ref 17592186516227 13194139535557 17592186045417 true] [17592186186378 1075 #edb/ref 17592186516322 13194139535557 17592186045417 true] [17592186186378 1075 #edb/ref 17592186516546 13194139535557 17592186045417 true] [17592186186378 1075 #edb/ref 17592186516552 13194139535557 17592186045417 true] [17592186186378 1075 #edb/ref 17592186516647 13194139535557 17592186045417 true] [17592186186378 1078 #edb/ref 17592186516552 13194139535557 17592186045417 true] [17592186188259 1038 #edb/ref 17592186516769 13194139535557 17592186045417 true] [17592186188259 1038 #edb/ref 17592186516828 13194139535557 17592186045417 true] [17592186188683 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535557 17592186045417 true] [17592186188683 1071 "Suppose we take a random sample of size $n = 100$ from a population where $60%$ of the population has a particular characteristic. Find a normal approximation for the probability that between $55%$ and $65%$ of the sample have the characteristic. You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.\n\n*Hint: $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139535557 17592186045417 false] [17592186188683 1071 "Suppose we take a random sample of size $n=100$ from a population where $60%$ of the population has a particular characteristic. Find a normal approximation for the probability that between $55%$ and $65%$ of the sample have the characteristic. You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.\n\n*Hint: $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*" 13194139535557 17592186045417 true] [17592186188683 1074 "Given a population in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere the sample size $n$ is much smaller than the population size, $np>5,$ and $n(1-p)>5.$\n\nIn our case\n\n- the sample size is $n=100,$ and\n\n- the population proportion is $p=0.6.$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nnp=100(0.6)=60>5\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nn(1-p)=100(1-0.6)=40>5,\n$$\n\nand $n=100$ is much smaller than the population size, we can apply the above approximation.\n\nThe sampling distribution of the sample proportion, in this case, can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\,p} & ∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.6,\\frac{0.6(1-0.6)}{100}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.6,0.0024).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe probability we wish to find is\n$P(0.55\\le \\overset{^}{\\,p}\\le 0.65).$\nSo, we convert $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0.55\\le \\overset{^}{\\,p}\\le 0.65) & =P(\\frac{0.55-0.6}{\\sqrt{0.0024}}\\le Z\\le \\frac{0.65-0.6}{\\sqrt{0.0024}}) \\\\  & ≈P(-1.02\\le Z\\le 1.02) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 1.02)-P(Z\\le -1.02) \\\\  & =Φ(1.02)-Φ(-1.02)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535557 17592186045417 true] [17592186193979 1078 #edb/ref 17592186193982 13194139535557 17592186045417 true] [17592186194381 1075 #edb/ref 17592186194382 13194139535557 17592186045417 false] [17592186194381 1075 #edb/ref 17592186194386 13194139535557 17592186045417 false] [17592186194381 1075 #edb/ref 17592186516525 13194139535557 17592186045417 true] [17592186194381 1075 #edb/ref 17592186516596 13194139535557 17592186045417 true] [17592186194381 1078 #edb/ref 17592186194383 13194139535557 17592186045417 true] [17592186198255 1038 #edb/ref 17592186516871 13194139535557 17592186045417 true] [17592186198368 1038 #edb/ref 17592186516795 13194139535557 17592186045417 true] [17592186198403 1038 #edb/ref 17592186516846 13194139535557 17592186045417 true] [17592186198403 1038 #edb/ref 17592186516848 13194139535557 17592186045417 true] [17592186198435 1038 #edb/ref 17592186516867 13194139535557 17592186045417 true] [17592186198558 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535557 17592186045417 true] [17592186198558 1071 "Given the vector field $F(x, y) = xi - yj$, which of the following statements must be true?\n\n1. $∇ \\cdot F = 0$\n2. $F$ is a source-free vector field\n3. $\\displaystyle \\int_(C)F \\cdot nds = 0$ along any piecewise-smooth curve $C$" 13194139535557 17592186045417 false] [17592186198558 1071 "Given the vector field $F(x,y)=x\\,i-y\\,j,$ which of the following statements must be true?\n\nI. $∇\\cdot F=0$\n\nII. $F$ is a source-free vector field\n\nIII. ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=0$ along any piecewise-smooth curve $C$" 13194139535557 17592186045417 true] [17592186198558 1074 "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field with simply connected domain $D⊆{R}^{2},$ then the following are equivalent:\n\n- $F$ is a source-free vector field.\n\n- $∇\\cdot F=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=0.$\n\n- The flux ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s$ along any closed curve $C⊂D$ is zero.\n\n- Flux is path-independent. That is, given any two curves ${C}_{1},{C}_{2}⊂D$ with the same start and endpoints, we have that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\n- There exists a stream function $ψ(x,y)$ for $F.$\n\nNote that $F(x,y)=x\\,i-y\\,j$ is a vector field with the simply connected domain $D={R}^{2},$ where $P(x,y)=x$ and $Q(x,y)=-y.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=1,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, we have that\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=1+(-1)=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. This follows from statement I.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $F$ is source-free, the result ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=0$ is guaranteed for closed curves only.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139535557 17592186045417 true] [17592186198625 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535557 17592186045417 true] [17592186198625 1071 "![](images/4f/4f9cd7d9e173410de891756ee2ef260a16677629dacbfc56ab5e6e207a9e40e4.png)\n\nWhat is the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,i+2y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,j$ across the curve $C$ shown above?\n\n*Hint: Start by checking whether $F$ is source-free.*" 13194139535557 17592186045417 true] [17592186198625 1071 "![](images/9c/9ceeaef97ccf45e8c73cb462f7057d4b60c7b99d576bd106911d74e72623e402.png)\n\nWhat is the flux of the vector field $F(x, y) = 2\\cos xi + 2y\\sin xj$ across the curve $C$ shown above.\n\n*Hint: Start by checking whether $F$ is source-free.*" 13194139535557 17592186045417 false] [17592186198625 1074 "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field with simply connected domain $D⊆{R}^{2},$ then the following are equivalent:\n\n- $F$ is a source-free vector field.\n\n- $∇\\cdot F=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=0.$\n\n- The flux ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s$ along any closed curve $C⊂D$ is zero.\n\n- Flux is path-independent. That is, given any two curves ${C}_{1},{C}_{2}⊂D$ with the same start and endpoints, we have that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\n- There exists a stream function $ψ(x,y)$ for $F.$\n\nFirst, notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $F$ is a source-free vector field. So, the flux of $F$ across any closed curve is zero.\nHence,\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=0.\n$$" 13194139535557 17592186045417 true] [17592186221431 1038 #edb/ref 17592186516859 13194139535557 17592186045417 true] [17592186221431 1038 #edb/ref 17592186516870 13194139535557 17592186045417 true] [17592186231405 1038 #edb/ref 17592186516819 13194139535557 17592186045417 true] [17592186231418 1038 #edb/ref 17592186516768 13194139535557 17592186045417 true] [17592186231418 1038 #edb/ref 17592186516829 13194139535557 17592186045417 true] [17592186231419 1038 #edb/ref 17592186516796 13194139535557 17592186045417 true] [17592186231419 1038 #edb/ref 17592186516813 13194139535557 17592186045417 true] [17592186231419 1038 #edb/ref 17592186516847 13194139535557 17592186045417 true] [17592186233597 1068 #edb/ref 17592186516732 13194139535557 17592186045417 true] [17592186234092 1068 #edb/ref 17592186516677 13194139535557 17592186045417 true] [17592186234301 1068 #edb/ref 17592186516717 13194139535557 17592186045417 true] [17592186296765 1075 #edb/ref 17592186516550 13194139535557 17592186045417 true] [17592186296765 1078 #edb/ref 17592186295437 13194139535557 17592186045417 false] [17592186296765 1078 #edb/ref 17592186516550 13194139535557 17592186045417 true] [17592186316716 1075 #edb/ref 17592186516414 13194139535557 17592186045417 true] [17592186316716 1078 #edb/ref 17592186316437 13194139535557 17592186045417 false] [17592186316716 1078 #edb/ref 17592186516414 13194139535557 17592186045417 true] [17592186338348 1075 #edb/ref 17592186336769 13194139535557 17592186045417 false] [17592186338348 1075 #edb/ref 17592186337192